PROPERTIES:

- The probability of reaching a state where A (B) does not knows a pair 
  while B (A) does knows a pair.

P=?[ true U !"knowA" & "knowB" {"init"} ]
P=?[ true U "knowA" & !"knowB" {"init"} ]

==========================================================================
- The expected number of bits that A needs to know a pair  once B (A) 
  knows a pair.

add rewards which is the minimum number of bits that the 
party requires in the case of B

formula costB = min(L-min(a1_0,a2_0),
                    L-min(a1_1,a2_1),
                    L-min(a1_2,a2_2),
                    L-min(a1_3,a2_3),
                    L-min(a1_4,a2_4),
                    L-min(a1_5,a2_5),
                    L-min(a1_6,a2_6),
                    L-min(a1_7,a2_7),
                    L-min(a1_8,a2_8),
                    L-min(a1_9,a2_9));

then change the protocol such that when the other party knows a pair we
jump out of the protocol and get the cost. Probably a number of ways of
doing this action or state based so need to have both?

property R=?[F ... ]

==========================================================================

- The expected number of messages from B (A) that A (B) needs to knows a 
  pair once B (A) knows a pair.

Add a reward of one to all transitions corresponding to a message when
the other party knows a pair. Best to do this with conditionals e.g.

rewards "messA"
	 [receiveA] phase=2..3 : ((kB & !kA)?1:0);
endrewards
rewards "messB"
	 [receiveB] phase=2..3 : ((!kB & kA)?1:0);
endrewards

then R{r}=?[ F phase=4 {"init"} ]

check no other ways.

==========================================================================
- The expected number of messages that need to be sent (by either party) 
  before A (B) knows a pair once B (A) knows a pair.

can extend this to other cases, giving the following reward structures:

rewards "time_A_knows"
	[receiveA] true : (!kA?1:0);
	[receiveB] true : (!kA?1:0);
endrewards

rewards "time_B_knows"
	[receiveA] true : (!kB?1:0);
	[receiveB] true : (!kB?1:0);
endrewards

rewards "time_either_knows"
	[receiveA] true : ((!kA & !kB)?1:0);
	[receiveB] true : ((!kA & !kB)?1:0);
endrewards

rewards "time_both_know"
	[receiveA] true : ((!kA | !kB)?1:0);
	[receiveB] true : ((!kA | !kB)?1:0);
endrewards

then R{r}=?[ F phase=4 {"init"} ]

it is the results for this property that changed when bits are combined into
a single message (if one makes this change to the model then the results
relating to the number of bits will be wrong if one tries to verify them as
above - the reward structure for that property would need to be changed)

